{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Before HaShem, saying, Behold, etc.</b> It is difficult, his saying <b>saying</b>, for to whom would HaShem say the words of Moses? And perhaps the verse intends to say that Moses did not offer this response for the purpose of avoiding going on this mission, but rather like one who complains about himself for not having succeeded, and this is its saying the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b> — meaning, that the sole purpose of the speech was the saying itself, not any purpose other than it, namely preventing the mission — and understand." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that the intent of his words is to say another saying, and it is understood from the speech through his saying ״הֵן בְּנֵי וְגוֹ׳ וַאֲנִי עֲרַל שְׂפָתָיִם״ — he means by this that HaShem should be his healer; and see in the verse ״הֵן בְּנֵי״ etc." "<b>Behold, the children of Israel, etc.</b> This kal va-chomer is justified according to what I explained in the verse ״מִקֹּצֶר רוּחַ״ — that they are words of Torah. Moses would rightly judge a kal va-chomer, for he did not consider that this was the cause. And if we say that Moses also knew that the reason they did not listen was on account of shortness of spirit, the kal va-chomer would be refuted." "Or perhaps he judges a kal va-chomer along these lines: that although the children of Israel are in shortness of spirit, they also possess two qualities — the first, that they are people of faith and acknowledge the kingdom of heaven; and the second, that the tidings are good and mighty tidings. And these two are not found in Pharaoh, for he denied the fundamental principle and said ״מִי ה׳״, heaven forfend, and furthermore they are matters that strike his eyes and pierce his heart. If so, the kal va-chomer is valid: and if the children of Israel, in whom these two aforementioned qualities exist, did not have HaShem incline their hearts to listen — and they did not listen because of some reason, namely that they have shortness of spirit — how would Pharaoh listen, who has two causes preventing him from listening: that he does not believe at all, and they are words that choke him? It is obvious that he would not listen even though he is not short of spirit. For Pharaoh's shortness of spirit, both regarding the words of Moses and regarding the words of the Sender, is greater than the shortness of spirit of Israel. And if there were power in the words of Moses to compel Pharaoh to listen to him, all the more so would the children of Israel have listened to him even given their shortness of spirit. And this is its saying <b>לֵאמֹר הֵן</b> etc. — meaning, even though one may raise the difficulty: what relevance does this have to the children of Israel, etc. — yet there is also grounds to say it in order to establish that structure as mentioned." "And his saying <b>and I am of uncircumcised</b> etc., meaning: there is yet another obstacle, namely that he is of uncircumcised lips, and Pharaoh will say: if the Elohim of the Hebrews is sovereign and omnipotent, why did He not heal Moses? And along the lines of this matter too, Pharaoh differs from Israel, in that he lacks faith altogether, so that he will seek to distance the faith and deny it, heaven forfend." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend that since HaShem was precise in His words that He said to him in the portion of Shemot (Exodus 4:16) concerning Aaron, ״וְדִבֶּר הוּא לְךָ אֶל הָעָם״ — from this you learn that Aaron is a mouth for him only to the people, but not to Pharaoh. And so you will find that the verse said (Exodus 4:30) concerning the first mission to the people, ״וַיְדַבֵּר אַהֲרֹן אֶת כָּל הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ אֶל מֹשֶׁה״, whereas when they spoke to Pharaoh it says (Exodus 5:1) ״וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֶל פַּרְעֹה״ — indicating that Moses would speak. And the reason it says ״וַיֹּאמְרוּ״ in the plural is that so is the rule for any two people, that when one speaks and one concurs they are considered as though both spoke. And if you would say that it follows this line — that Aaron spoke and Moses concurred — from the fact that we see the verse was precise in saying concerning speech to the people, ״וַיְדַבֵּר אַהֲרֹן״ exactly, and likewise in the words of HaShem we find that He said only ״וְדִבֶּר הוּא לְךָ אֶל הָעָם״ — this will prove that these considerations justify our position. And accordingly, the force of an a fortiori argument is his saying ״וַאֲנִי עֲרַל שְׂפָתָיִם״, for this aspect applies only to Pharaoh, since Moses himself speaks — unlike speaking to the people, where Aaron is the one who speaks. And perhaps it is for this reason that He was precise to say regarding Israel the word <b>אֵלַי</b> while regarding Pharaoh He said <b>יִשְׁמָעֵנִי</b>, for Israel do not hear from his mouth literally but from the mouth of Aaron, whereas Pharaoh would hear from Moses himself. And keep this detail in mind to understand the holy statements nearby."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:25:26.500578418Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}